    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I fixed `mock_openai()`, and `pytest -q` now passes (2 passed).\n\n**Cause:** openai 3.19.2 no longer uses `httpx` for its requests. It sends them through a separate package, `httpx2`. respx only patches `httpx`, so its routes never saw the requests, and nothing reported that.\n\n**Fix (only in `tests/fakes.py`):**\n- For the duration of the `with` block, the helper patches `httpx2.Client._transport_for_url` on the class. It's a private httpx2 method, the same kind respx patches in `httpx`. Any request under `https://api.openai.com/v1/` then goes to an `httpx2.MockTransport`, which answers `POST /chat/completions` with the canned replies (and a 400 once they run out). Any other OpenAI path gets a 404 instead of reaching the network. Requests to other hosts are unaffected.\n- Because the patch is on the class, it covers app.digest's cached client and clients created before or inside the block. The patch is undone when the block exits.\n- The respx mock is still there, so clients built on a legacy `httpx.Client` are also caught. The canned-reply logic is shared between both paths.\n- If a future httpx2 version removes the method, the patch raises `AttributeError` instead of silently letting requests through.\n\nThe interface is unchanged: same signature, and it still yields the list of request bodies.\n\n**Checks:** besides the test suite, I ran a scratch script. A client created before the block and one created inside it both got the canned replies, and a request beyond the replies got the 400. After the block, that script's request went out to the real OpenAI API with a dummy key `\"x\"` and got a 401. That confirms nothing is intercepted after the block, but it did send a real request.\n\nI couldn't delete the scratch file, because the `rm` needed approval. Please delete `_chk.py` from the project root."
  },
